  "history": "Bus is the end of a Latin word. In Latin, omnibus means ‘for all, for everyone’. In the 1820s, horse-drawn coaches in France that took any passenger along a fixed route were called voitures omnibus, ‘vehicles for all’. In 1829 George Shillibeer brought the idea to London, and English took the name: omnibus. People soon cut it to bus, at first written with an apostrophe: ’bus. So the word bus is really just a Latin ending, -bus. The plural is buses. Today there are also minibuses and trolleybuses, and in a computer a bus is the set of wires that carries data to all its parts.",
  "historyEinfach": "Bus comes from Latin. Omnibus means ‘for all people’. About 200 years ago, there were big coaches with horses in France. Everybody could use them. They were called omnibus. Then London had them too. People said bus. It was shorter.",
